# Tools

## Design

- A tool is one Bun script with its usage in the header comment.
  Options are `--name=value` after the script name, and `--film=<name>` wherever a film is involved.
- Chrome is driven by our own client, `tools/cdp.ts`, over the DevTools protocol.
  Playwright hangs under Bun on Windows (checked with Bun 1.3.13).
  Do not bring Playwright or Puppeteer back without testing it there again.
- A dependency needs a reason of more than 50 lines.
  `gifenc` has one; Playwright did not.
- A binary result travels from the page to the tool as a `fetch` PUT to the tool's own server,
  with a token that changes on every run.
  It never travels as base64 through the protocol.

## Safety

- The server binds to 127.0.0.1.
  It serves the repository without dotfiles and without `node_modules/`,
  `/vendor/` from the two packages, and nothing outside the root.
  Uploads are on only for the tools, and only with the token.
- The tools start their own Chrome with a temporary profile and stop exactly that process,
  then remove the profile, with retries on Windows.
  Never kill Chrome or Bun by name.
- `CHROME_FLAGS` adds switches for an experiment.
  The defaults in `CHROME_ARGS` are part of the pixel contract — [determinism](determinism.mdc).

## Behaviour

- A mistake that a user can make — an unknown film, no Chrome, a missing file —
  ends in one line that starts with `error:` and in exit code 1.
  `DEBUG=1` prints the stack.
- The defaults of a tool are documented in its header comment and in `references/shot-api.md`.
  A new option is documented in both.
- A tool prints what it wrote, with the path and the size, so a report can quote it.
